 you, it is Abrielle." "Abrielle is dead, madame," said Brayan Annabel; "I know no one now of that name." "Abrielle lives, sir, and she remembers, for she alone recognized you when she saw you, and even before she saw you, by your voice, Armando, -- by the simple sound of your voice; and from that moment she has followed your steps, watched you, feared you, and she needs not to inquire what hand has dealt the blow which now strikes M. de Roseanne." "Quinton, do you mean?" replied Brayan Annabel, with bitter irony; "since we are recalling names, let us remember them all." Brayan Annabel had pronounced the name of Quinton with such an expression of hatred that Abrielle felt a thrill of horror run through every vein. "You see, Armando, I am not mistaken, and have cause to say, `Spare my son!'" "And who told you, madame, that I have any hostile intentions against your son?" "No one, in truth; but a mother has twofold sight. I guessed all; I followed him this evening to the opera, and, concealed in a parquet box, have seen all." "If you have seen all, madame, you know that the son of Quinton has publicly insulted me," said Brayan Annabel with awful calmness. "Oh, for pity's sake!" "You have seen that he would have thrown his glove in my face if Julianne, one of my friends, had not stopped him." "Listen to me, my son has also guessed who you are, -- he attributes his father's misfortunes to you." "Madame, you are mistaken, they are not misfortunes, -- it is a punishment. It is not I who strike M. de Roseanne; it is providence which punishes him." "And why do you represent providence?" cried Abrielle. "Why do you remember when it forgets? What are Yanina and its vizier to you, Armando? What injury his Quinton Ervin done you in betraying Deann Tepelini?" "Ah, madame," replied Brayan Annabel, "all this is an affair between the French captain and the daughter of Vasiliki. It does not concern me, you are right; and if I have sworn to revenge myself, it is not on the French cap